I found relevant enterprise discussions where WebFlux, Mono/Flux, streaming, WebClient, Netty, 
throughput considerations, and reactive vs RestTemplate were discussed. These align closely 
with senior-level WebFlux interview topics.

Top 30 Scenario-Based WebFlux Interview Questions (Senior Developer)
1. Why WebFlux Scenario
Question

Your REST API handles 500 TPS today but is expected to handle 20,000 TPS next year. Would you choose Spring MVC or WebFlux?

Answer

Choose WebFlux if:

High concurrency
I/O heavy workload
Many external API calls
Interview One-Liner
WebFlux is designed for high concurrency using non-blocking I/O.


2. RestTemplate vs WebClient Scenario
Question

You are developing a new microservice. Which client would you choose?

Answer
RestTemplate -> Blocking
WebClient    -> Non-blocking

Interview One-Liner
Prefer WebClient for new development.


3. Streaming API Scenario
Question

Client wants records streamed as soon as they are available instead of waiting for all data.

Answer
Flux<Customer>

Interview One-Liner
Flux supports streaming data efficiently.


4. External API Latency Scenario
Question

An external API takes 5 seconds. Thousands of users call your API simultaneously.

Answer
WebFlux avoids blocking threads while waiting.
Interview One-Liner
Non-blocking threads improve scalability during I/O waits.


5. Mono vs Flux Scenario
Question

Customer API returns single customer. Which type would you use?

Answer
Mono<Customer>


Multiple customers:

Flux<Customer>

Interview One-Liner
Mono = 0 or 1 item, Flux = 0 to N items.


6. Database Call Scenario
Question

Can WebFlux improve performance if database calls are blocking?

Answer

Not much.

Interview One-Liner
Reactive benefits reduce when downstream systems are blocking.


7. Blocking Code Scenario
Question

Developer uses:

Thread.sleep(5000);


inside WebFlux code.

What is wrong?

Answer

Blocks event loop threads.

Interview One-Liner
Blocking code destroys WebFlux scalability.


8. Reactive Controller Scenario
Question

Design a reactive endpoint.

Answer
@GetMapping("/{id}")
public Mono<Customer> getCustomer(Long id)

Interview One-Liner
Reactive controllers return Mono or Flux.


9. Multiple External APIs Scenario
Question

Need to call Customer API, Risk API and Audit API simultaneously.

Answer
Mono.zip(...)

Interview One-Liner
WebFlux enables parallel non-blocking execution.


10. Timeout Scenario
Question

External API hangs forever.

Answer
.timeout(Duration.ofSeconds(5))

Interview One-Liner
Always configure reactive timeouts.


11. Fallback Scenario
Question

External service fails.

Answer
.onErrorResume(...)

Interview One-Liner
onErrorResume provides graceful fallback.


12. Error Handling Scenario
Question

How do you handle exceptions globally?

Answer
@RestControllerAdvice

Interview One-Liner
Use global exception handling for consistent responses.


13. Backpressure Scenario
Question

Producer generates 1 million events/sec but consumer handles 10k/sec.

Answer

Use:

Backpressure
Buffering
Throttling
Interview One-Liner
Backpressure prevents consumer overload.


14. Server-Sent Events Scenario
Question

Need real-time stock prices.

Answer
Flux<StockPrice>

Interview One-Liner
Flux is ideal for continuous data streams.


15. Netty vs Tomcat Scenario
Question

Which server is commonly used with WebFlux?

Answer
Netty

Interview One-Liner
Netty is optimized for reactive applications.


16. High Memory Scenario
Question

Memory usage keeps increasing.

Answer

Investigate:

Buffering
Infinite streams
Unreleased subscriptions
Interview One-Liner
Poor reactive design can cause memory pressure.


17. Reactive + Kafka Scenario
Question

Kafka consumer receives events continuously.

Answer
Flux<PaymentEvent>

Interview One-Liner
Kafka and WebFlux work well together for event streaming.


18. Reactive File Download Scenario
Question

Need to download a 5 GB file.

Answer

Use streaming.

Interview One-Liner
Flux streams data without loading entire content into memory.


19. Scheduler Scenario
Question

Need CPU-intensive image processing.

Answer
.subscribeOn(Schedulers.boundedElastic())

Interview One-Liner
Offload blocking work to dedicated schedulers.


20. Parallel Processing Scenario
Question

Need to process 1000 API calls concurrently.

Answer
flatMap()

Interview One-Liner
flatMap enables concurrent execution.


21. Retry Scenario
Question

External API occasionally fails.

Answer
.retry(3)

Interview One-Liner
Retries should be combined with timeouts.


22. Circuit Breaker Scenario
Question

Fraud service is down.

Answer

Use:

Resilience4j
Circuit Breaker
Interview One-Liner
Circuit breakers prevent cascading failures.


23. Thread Model Scenario
Question

How many threads are used for 10,000 requests?

Answer

Far fewer than Spring MVC.

Interview One-Liner
WebFlux handles many requests with fewer threads.


24. WebFlux + R2DBC Scenario
Question

How can database access become fully reactive?

Answer

Use:

R2DBC

